                <h2>Understanding Classes and Objects</h2>
                <p>
                    In C#, a class is a blueprint or template for creating objects. It defines the structure and behavior that
                    objects of that class will have. Think of a class as a cookie cutter and objects as the cookies made from it.
                </p>

                <div class="card bg-light mb-4">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h5 class="card-title"><i class="fas fa-lightbulb text-warning me-2"></i>Real-World Analogy</h5>
                        <p class="card-text">A "Car" class defines what a car is (color, model, speed) and what it can do (start, stop, accelerate). Each actual car object has specific values for these properties.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2>Basic Class Syntax</h2>
                <pre><code>// Simple class definition
public class Person
{
    // Fields (data)
    public string Name;
    public int Age;
    
    // Method (behavior)
    public void Introduce()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Hi, I'm {Name} and I'm {Age} years old.");
    }
}</code></pre>

                <h2>Creating Objects</h2>
                <pre><code>// Creating an instance of the class
Person person1 = new Person();
person1.Name = "John";
person1.Age = 25;
person1.Introduce();

// Using object initializer
Person person2 = new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 30 };
person2.Introduce();</code></pre>

                <h2>Class Components</h2>
                <h3>1. Fields</h3>
                <p>Variables that hold data for the class:</p>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    public string FirstName;
    public string LastName;
    public int StudentId;
    public double GPA;
}</code></pre>

                <h3>2. Properties</h3>
                <p>Provide controlled access to fields:</p>
                <pre><code>public class Product
{
    private string name;
    private decimal price;
    
    public string Name
    {
        get { return name; }
        set { name = value; }
    }
    
    public decimal Price
    {
        get { return price; }
        set 
        { 
            if (value < 0)
                throw new ArgumentException("Price cannot be negative");
            price = value; 
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

                <h3>3. Methods</h3>
                <p>Define behavior for the class:</p>
                <pre><code>public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    public int Multiply(int a, int b)
    {
        return a * b;
    }
}</code></pre>

                <h2>Complete Example</h2>
                <pre><code>using System;

public class Book
{
    // Fields
    private string title;
    private string author;
    private int pages;
    
    // Properties
    public string Title
    {
        get { return title; }
        set { title = value; }
    }
    
    public string Author
    {
        get { return author; }
        set { author = value; }
    }
    
    public int Pages
    {
        get { return pages; }
        set 
        { 
            if (value < 0)
                throw new ArgumentException("Pages cannot be negative");
            pages = value; 
        }
    }
    
    // Method
    public void DisplayInfo()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Title: {Title}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Author: {Author}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Pages: {Pages}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Reading time: {CalculateReadingTime()} hours");
    }
    
    private double CalculateReadingTime()
    {
        // Assuming average reading speed of 50 pages per hour
        return (double)Pages / 50;
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Book myBook = new Book();
        myBook.Title = "C# Programming";
        myBook.Author = "John Doe";
        myBook.Pages = 350;
        myBook.DisplayInfo();
    }
}</code></pre>

                <div class="card bg-info bg-opacity-10 mb-4">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h5 class="card-title"><i class="fas fa-key me-2"></i>Key Takeaway</h5>
                        <p class="card-text">A class is a blueprint that combines data (fields/properties) and behavior (methods) into a single unit. Objects are instances of classes.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2>Access Modifiers</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>public</strong> - Accessible from anywhere</li>
                    <li><strong>private</strong> - Accessible only within the class</li>
                    <li><strong>protected</strong> - Accessible within class and derived classes</li>
                    <li><strong>internal</strong> - Accessible within the same assembly</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Static vs Instance</h2>
                <pre><code>public class MathHelper
{
    // Static method - called on the class, not an instance
    public static int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    // Instance method - called on an object
    public int Multiply(int a, int b)
    {
        return a * b;
    }
}

// Usage
int sum = MathHelper.Add(5, 3); // Static call
MathHelper helper = new MathHelper();
int product = helper.Multiply(5, 3); // Instance call</code></pre>

